const video = document.createElement('video');
video.style.width = '200px';
video.style.height = '150px';
document.body.appendChild(video);

video.addEventListener('click', async () => {
  const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
  canvas.width = video.offsetWidth;
  canvas.height = video.offsetHeight;
  const context = canvas.getContext('2d');

  // Take a screenshot of the webcam
  const image = await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    video.captureStream().then((stream) => {
      context.drawImage(stream, 0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
      resolve(canvas.toDataURL('image/png'));
    }).catch((error) => reject(error));
  });

  // Display the captured image (you can update the image based on user actions)
  document.querySelector('.image').innerHTML = `<img src="${image}" alt="Captured Image">`;

  // Optionally, you can handle image capture based on your app logic
  // ...
});
```

**Explanation:**

1. **HTML Setup:** The script utilizes a `<video>` element to mimic a webcam capture. This video element will dynamically capture and display the webcam feed.
2. **Event Listener:** An event listener on the `video` element triggers when the user clicks on it. This action initiates the image capture process.
3. **Canvas Capture:**
   - A canvas element is dynamically created with the same dimensions as the video.
   - The `captureStream()` method of the video element captures the webcam stream.
   - The `drawImage()` method of the canvas context draws the webcam stream onto the canvas, capturing a snapshot.
4. **Image Data:**
   - The `canvas.toDataURL()` method is used to obtain the base64 encoded data representing the captured image as a data URL.
5. **Image Display:** The captured image data is rendered in an existing image element in your application. 

